<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · river</p><h1 class="enc-title">Seine</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Seine is a river of northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a>, about 777 km long, which rises on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/langres">Langres</a> plateau in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/burgundy">Burgundy</a> and flows through <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/troyes">Troyes</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paris">Paris</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rouen">Rouen</a> to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/english-channel">English Channel</a> at Le Havre. Its basin was home to Gallic peoples such as the Parisii and Senones, and as the artery of Paris it made the region the political and economic heart of France. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vikings">Viking</a> fleets used it to raid deep inland in the 9th century.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/seine.jpg" alt="Seine" width="320" height="213" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Mortimer62 · CC BY-SA 2.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Bercy,_Paris_01.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
